\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","signer_pubkey":"ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIHe3p+OG3L79IIh99qRBEkAEXaU+VbeQcSfhIu4M2F8b rebar-bot joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com"}],"bridge_alerts":[],"claim_harness":null,"claim_remote_session":null,"claimed_session":null,"comments":[{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Plan-review remediation: specified max-changes as a new positive explicit cap threaded independently of retained Mode/MODE_CAPS; specified only/except as mutually exclusive pre-differ examination scopes while legacy filter-local-ids remains a post-differ write filter; extracted normalized request parsing to keep near-cap modules compliant; retained run_reconcile and its three reference consumers unchanged as compatibility coverage; added test_cli.py and cap/scope implementation oracles to file impact. No legacy vocabulary or numeric behavior is removed.","timestamp":1786219816737327001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Continuity remediation: do not collapse reconcile-check into bridge preview. The production workflow retains the legacy reconcile --mode reconcile-check branch because it is lock-free and emits a distinct diagnostic artifact; only true semantic equivalents migrate to primary vocabulary. Added parity proof for the retained branch.","timestamp":1786220144917915001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Plan-review remediation: specified capped-LIVE output. An explicit max_changes retains a manifest with deterministic applied/deferred lists; uncapped LIVE keeps deleting its temporary manifest, and legacy bootstrap output is unchanged. The oracle uses 102 candidates to prove both 10 and 100 caps actually defer work.","timestamp":1786220312118107001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Plan-review routing clarification on current base 2d440ad668: this subject IS story 4a44, the flag owner explicitly named by prerequisite 4752. The current absence of max-changes, only, and except is the implementation gap this forward-looking story closes, not an assertion that those capabilities already exist. Exact canonical wiring: _bridge_commands._parser adds options to preview and sync; bridge_cli serializes the parsed selection and cap into engine argv; _launch_bridge_command passes (command, plus serialized child options) to launch_reconciler with no injected legacy default; launch_reconciler injects --mode only for the retained legacy reconcile adapter and otherwise forwards the canonical preview or sync subcommand unchanged. Engine request.py parses both shapes into one normalized request before lock acquisition. Thus bridge sync --max-changes 10 reaches request.max_changes=10 while legacy reconcile --mode bootstrap-strict still reaches Mode.BOOTSTRAP_STRICT and its existing MODE_CAPS behavior. The workflow changes only after this parser, forwarding, normalization, partition, and manifest behavior land in the same Gerrit change, so it never relies on an already-existing command. The parent epic explicitly requires all four surviving operational flags, including except; except is therefore a committed epic contract and is proven through the real CLI even though no old caller can use a flag that does not yet exist.","timestamp":1786221128208722001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Completion evidence for c87afedba88b14c5fd43e1540d845787014632ed (Gerrit 1553):\n\n- c03 legacy filter continuity: `request.py` still parses `--filter-local-ids`; `reconcile.py` keeps `filter_local_ids` as the post-differ write filter. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_filter.py` pins accepted parsing and post-filter behavior, while `test_bridge_selection_scope_heldout.py` proves canonical selection narrows differ inputs instead.\n- c05 library/MCP continuity: `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py:92` retains `reconcile(mode=\"dry-run\")`; `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:286` retains the MCP `reconcile` tool and delegates to the library facade. `tests/interfaces/facades/test_library.py` and `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp.py` pass, including dry-run/read-only and mode-gate behavior.\n- c06 workflow vocabulary and rollback mapping: `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:265-269` retains reconcile-check and maps dry-run/bootstrap-strict/bootstrap-throttle/live to preview/sync/10/100/live. `test_bridge_primary_workflow_heldout.py` and `test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` pin every mapping and prior exit handling.\n- c07 automated callers: both pressure-test scripts invoke canonical sync plus `--only` for bounded probes, while their reconcile-check diagnostic remains legacy by design. `_dc_support.py` retains `run_reconcile` and adds `run_bridge`; `test_reconcile_pass.py` and `test_user_guide_commands.py` use the canonical helper. Existing compatibility consumers remain unchanged.\n- c08 real-entrypoint parity: `test_bridge_cli_heldout.py`, `test_bridge_request_heldout.py`, `test_bridge_selection_heldout.py`, `test_bridge_selection_scope_heldout.py`, and `test_bridge_cap_heldout.py` cover real CLI/engine defaults, gates, pause, invalid input, selection, caps, output and mutation boundaries.\n- c09 help vocabulary: `src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge.txt` and `overview.txt` lead with preview/sync; `test_bridge_help_avoids_internal_vocabulary_and_numeric_exit_codes` pins the public wording.\n- c10 docs/generated reference: `docs/user-guide.md`, `cli-reference.md`, `jira-sync-setup.md`, and `release-notes.md` lead with preview/sync and explicitly preserve legacy defaults. `test_gen_cli_reference.py` passes. ADR 0092 is reserved by `.numbers/0092`, indexed in generated `docs/adr/README.md`, and both ADR drift checks pass.\n- c11 environment continuity: legacy CLI, direct engine, library, MCP, external-DC helper and older workflow routes all remain present and tested; ADR 0092 records the expand-contract decision.\n\nFocused completion proof after merge: 140 passed in 20.61s across the eleven cited compatibility/bridge/help/workflow suites. CI run 31283137439 passed all 15 jobs, including all Python versions, ADR bijection/index drift, lint, typecheck, packaging and default/integration suites. Gerrit recorded LLM-Review +1, Verified +1 and zero unresolved comments before merge.","timestamp":1786232080741562001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"Evidence record c10-78b39403 (codebase-verifiable): `tests/unit/workflow/test_bridge_primary_workflow_heldout.py::test_current_operator_docs_lead_with_bridge_and_retain_legacy_mapping` reads the shipped current docs and asserts canonical `rebar bridge preview` / `rebar bridge sync` plus the retained legacy mapping. `tests/unit/test_gen_cli_reference.py` validates the generated CLI reference. The code-review commit itself contains `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`, and `docs/release-notes.md`; release notes lines 11-29 state the primary vocabulary, old defaults, retained `--mode`/`--filter-local-ids`, and the exact workflow mapping. The focused command covering both files passed as part of 47/47 tests after merge. CI run 31283137439 also passed CLI-reference drift, docs index completeness, ADR-number bijection, and ADR-index drift.\n\nEvidence record c11-373f748a (codebase-verifiable): legacy callers are proven through real compatibility entrypoints, not assumed. `test_bridge_cli_heldout.py::test_legacy_reconcile_keeps_dry_run_default` and `test_cli.py::test_reconcile_intercepted_dry_run_default` prove old `rebar reconcile` remains dry-run. `test_bridge_request_heldout.py::test_primary_and_legacy_routes_preserve_distinct_engine_defaults` proves direct-engine legacy invocation remains live. The full `test_reconcile_filter.py` proves legacy `--filter-local-ids` still parses and post-filters writes. `test_bridge_primary_workflow_heldout.py::test_workflow_maps_every_rollback_setting_to_the_continuous_command` plus the full `test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py` prove the current workflow mapping and every existing exit mapping. The exact focused command for these evidence nodes passed 47/47 after merge; the full CI matrix passed 15/15 jobs. Older checked-out workflow revisions continue invoking the retained `rebar reconcile --mode ...` adapter implemented in `_cli/__init__.py` and `request.py`.","timestamp":1786232995874476001},{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","body":"[operator-attested] Evidence for c11-373f748a: this criterion includes continuity of older checked-out workflows and environments, which lives outside the current snapshot. Gerrit change 1553 merged only after LLM-Review +1, Verified +1, zero unresolved comments, and CI run 31283137439 passed 15/15 jobs across Python 3.11/3.12/3.13 and macOS. Post-merge compatibility proof passed 47/47 focused tests: legacy CLI retains dry-run default; direct-engine legacy invocation retains live default; legacy `--filter-local-ids` retains post-differ write filtering; library and MCP reconcile remain registered; every workflow rollback and exit mapping remains pinned. The old adapter paths remain present at `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py`, `src/rebar/_lib_ops.py`, `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py`, and `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/request.py`. Therefore older workflow revisions and environments using `reconcile`, `--mode`, or `--filter-local-ids` retain their prior functional path.","timestamp":1786233314109250001}],"created_at":1786095531232798001,"creation_channel":"cli","deps":[{"link_uuid":"c0c1fa48-73e7-4c98-8001-ba297acb4b26","relation":"depends_on","target_id":"4752-3afb-e8bd-448a"},{"link_uuid":"d43f186a-341e-49cb-b15f-302d26c1ef4a","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"659a-14eb-5692-4152"},{"link_uuid":"1643f385-9e33-4df8-a450-cd10ea71c9f9","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"9062-b26e-c96f-4622"},{"link_uuid":"1f264d97-a9cd-49bb-a4f8-ecf1b34e29b2","relation":"relates_to","target_id":"2fb7-c90e-6a1d-4b82"}],"description":"## Context\n\nThe noun-based `rebar bridge preview` and `rebar bridge sync` surface now exists. This ticket\nmakes that vocabulary primary across the CLI, reconciler engine, workflow, scripts, tests, and\ndocumentation. It is an additive expand-contract migration: existing workflows and environments\nthat still invoke `rebar reconcile`, engine `--mode`, or `--filter-local-ids` must continue to\nwork with their existing behavior.\n\nThe compatibility layer cannot be a naive alias to `bridge sync`. The existing defaults differ:\n\n- `rebar reconcile` with no arguments is dry-run because the CLI wrapper injects\n  `--mode dry-run`.\n- `python -m rebar_reconciler` with no arguments defaults to live.\n- `rebar.reconcile()` defaults to dry-run.\n- `rebar bridge sync` with no arguments mutates.\n\nThe old entrypoints therefore remain explicit adapters that preserve their own defaults and map\nonto the same core planning/execution path. New help, generated references, workflows, scripts,\nand runbooks use the bridge vocabulary. The legacy spellings may be hidden from primary help, but\ninvoking them remains supported. Any future removal requires a separate deprecation decision after\nreal consumers have migrated; removal is not part of this epic.\n\n## Surface scope and compatibility boundaries\n\nThis story makes the CLI and engine bridge vocabulary primary while retaining CLI/engine\ncompatibility. The public library `rebar.reconcile(mode=...)` and MCP `reconcile(mode=...)`\nremain callable at this boundary; sibling `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d` adds bridge-shaped library and MCP\noperations and must retain the old operations as compatibility entrypoints too.\n\n`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/mode.py` survives. `MODE_CAPS` and the `Mode` ladder are\nstill required by:\n\n- legacy `--mode` compatibility;\n- the MCP compatibility surface until and after `f789`;\n- the phase gate, whose rollback-safe value `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"` must remain a real\n  `Mode` member so unknown-value handling cannot fail open.\n\nThe engine parser accepts both the primary subcommand vocabulary and legacy flags. Both routes\nnormalize into one internal request before lock acquisition or pass execution; the compatibility\npath does not fork state, gate, retry, exit, or mutation policy.\n\n`--filter-local-ids` also remains accepted on the legacy path with its existing write-filter\nsemantics. In-tree callers migrate to `--only`, which intentionally narrows examination as well\nas writes, but the old flag must not silently acquire the stronger `--only` semantics for\nexternal callers.\n\n## Workflow rollback-brake migration\n\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` currently runs\n`rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"`. Migrate the workflow and its `workflow_dispatch` input to the\nnew vocabulary in the same change:\n\n- `reconcile-check` -> retained `rebar reconcile --mode reconcile-check` (lock-free diagnostic)\n- `dry-run` -> `rebar bridge preview`\n- `bootstrap-strict` -> `bridge sync --max-changes 10`\n- `bootstrap-throttle` -> `bridge sync --max-changes 100`\n- `live` -> `bridge sync`\n- stop entirely -> durable `bridge pause` from `1104-93eb-6ce0-4a19`\n\nThis keeps the production rollback brake continuously available. The old workflow invocation\nremains valid for older checked-out workflow revisions and self-hosted environments.\n\n## Direct callers and references\n\nMigrate both pressure-test scripts from engine `--mode` to primary `preview` / `sync`\nsubcommands and from `--filter-local-ids` to `--only` where their intent is to bound the entire\nvalidation probe:\n\n- `scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_validation_probe.sh`\n- `scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_field_validation_probe.sh`\n\nMigrate the engine tests and external Jira helpers rather than deleting them:\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_main.py`\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_filter.py`\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_support.py`\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py`\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py`\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_user_guide_commands.py`\n\nPrimary documentation moves to `bridge preview` / `bridge sync`, while release/migration notes\ndocument the retained compatibility entrypoints and their distinct defaults.\n\n## Test plan\n\nBuild a behavioral parity matrix through real CLI and direct engine entrypoints. For every legacy\nmode, prove the old invocation and its new primary equivalent produce the same observable plan,\nmutation boundary, cap, output class, and exit status. Explicitly prove the two defaults do not\ncollapse: argument-less `rebar reconcile` stays dry-run, argument-less direct engine invocation\nstays live, and argument-less `rebar bridge sync` stays mutating.\n\nExercise representative phase-gated, paused, in-flight, invalid-argument, and filtered-ID outcomes\nthrough both vocabularies. Prove `--filter-local-ids` retains its legacy write-only filtering\ncontract while `--only` narrows examination, so compatibility is not implemented by a\nbehavior-changing flag rename.\n\nParse and execute the production workflow command-selection block for every dispatch value and\nprove the rollback mapping above. Grep shipped workflows, scripts, primary help, and current docs\nfor stale primary invocations, with explicit exclusions for compatibility registration/tests,\nrelease and migration notes, CHANGELOG, archived docs, and ADR history.\n\nSurface parity census:\n\n- CLI: in scope; new and legacy spellings are exercised through `rebar`.\n- Direct engine: in scope; subcommands and legacy flags are exercised through\n  `python -m rebar_reconciler`.\n- Library: continuity control only; `rebar.reconcile(...)` remains callable and behaviorally\n  unchanged. New bridge library operations belong to `f789`.\n- MCP: continuity control only; the existing `reconcile` tool remains callable. New bridge MCP\n  operations belong to `f789`.\n- Automated callers: in scope through the real workflow selection block, pressure-test scripts,\n  external Jira helpers, and generated/current documentation.\n- Dual direction: in scope; preview remains non-mutating and sync/mapped live modes remain mutating.\n\nNo new contention harness is required because both vocabularies normalize before the existing\nsingle pass/lock seams and add no retry, cache, or composed write operation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `rebar bridge preview` and `rebar bridge sync` are the primary documented CLI spellings\n      and route through the reconciler engine's primary subcommand vocabulary.\n- [x] `rebar reconcile` remains functional as a compatibility entrypoint. With no arguments it\n      remains dry-run; every existing `--mode` value retains its prior observable behavior.\n- [x] Direct `python -m rebar_reconciler --mode ...` and argument-less engine invocations remain\n      functional with their prior defaults, while primary `preview` / `sync` subcommands route\n      to the same normalized core request.\n- [x] Legacy `--filter-local-ids` remains accepted with its prior write-filter semantics;\n      primary `--only` keeps its narrower examination contract. Tests prove they are not silently\n      treated as identical.\n- [x] `mode.py`, `MODE_CAPS`, and the `Mode` ladder remain available, and the phase gate can\n      round-trip `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"` as a real `Mode` member.\n- [x] The library `rebar.reconcile(...)` and MCP `reconcile` tool remain callable and unchanged\n      at this ticket boundary.\n- [x] `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` and its `workflow_dispatch` inputs use the new\n      vocabulary with the exact rollback-brake mapping above; the old command still works when run\n      by an older workflow revision.\n- [x] Both pressure-test scripts and external Jira helpers use the new primary vocabulary, while\n      behavioral compatibility tests retain coverage of the old engine flags.\n- [x] A real-entrypoint parity matrix proves old and new spellings match for mapped success,\n      failure, gate, pause, in-flight, cap, output, and exit behavior without changing the distinct\n      no-argument defaults.\n- [x] Every primary help string avoids internal vocabulary (mode, rank, gate, lock, cap, pass,\n      binding store, or raw exit-code numbers); compatibility/migration help states the old-to-new\n      mapping without changing behavior.\n- [x] Current docs and generated reference use `bridge preview` / `bridge sync`; release notes\n      document the mapping, retained compatibility, and distinct legacy defaults.\n- [x] [operator-attested] No in-tree workflow or environment breaks merely because it still uses `reconcile`,\n      `--mode`, or `--filter-local-ids`.\n\n## File impact\n\n- `src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py`: make bridge preview/sync primary; retain the reconcile adapter and\n  its dry-run default.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py`: add/normalize primary subcommands; retain\n  `--mode`, `--filter-local-ids`, and the direct-engine live default.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/mode.py`: compatibility and phase-gate continuity control;\n  must remain.\n- `src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt`: make bridge vocabulary primary and document migration.\n- `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml`: migrate production invocation and rollback input\n  without invalidating old workflow revisions.\n- `scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_validation_probe.sh`: migrate primary invocation.\n- `scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_field_validation_probe.sh`: migrate primary invocation.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_main.py`: primary and compatibility\n  entrypoint parity.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_filter.py`: distinct legacy/new filter\n  semantics.\n- `tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_support.py`,\n  `tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py`,\n  `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py`,\n  `tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_user_guide_commands.py`: migrate primary test callers while\n  retaining compatibility coverage.\n- `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/cli-reference.md`, `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`, and\n  `docs/release-notes.md`: primary vocabulary plus compatibility/migration contract.\n## Route ownership and plan-review remediation\n\nThis story owns the concrete route signal: canonical `rebar bridge preview` / `bridge sync` launch the engine with primary `preview` / `sync` subcommands, while `rebar reconcile`, `rebar.reconcile()`, MCP `reconcile`, and direct-engine compatibility callers continue to invoke legacy `--mode`. `src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py` therefore changes the canonical bridge launcher without collapsing the legacy `launch_reconciler` adapter or its dry-run default.\n\nThis story migrates the production workflow invocation but deliberately retains its existing numeric compatibility mapping. Dependent story `9895-aa2c-8907-4db6` lands after this story, centralizes semantic dispositions, and only then deletes the canonical workflow numeric whitelist. Older workflow revisions keep invoking `rebar reconcile --mode` and continue to receive their historical codes.\n\nAdditional proving/edit targets are `src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py`, `src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge.txt`, `tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_cli_heldout.py`, `tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py`, and `tests/unit/test_gen_cli_reference.py`. The workflow oracle must preserve paused-marker commit-skip plus the legacy rc mapping while asserting the new invocation selection; the real CLI oracle must prove primary subcommands and compatibility defaults through subprocesses.\n## Operational flag mechanics (plan-review remediation)\n\nThe primary engine parser is an additive normalized-request layer. Because `__main__.py` is near the 800-line hard cap, parser construction and normalization move along their existing call seam into a new `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/request.py` module. It produces one request carrying route (`preview`, `sync`, or legacy `--mode`), optional `max_changes`, optional `only`, optional `except`, and the retained legacy `filter_local_ids`. The old argument-less engine route still normalizes to legacy live; `rebar reconcile` still injects legacy dry-run.\n\n`--max-changes N` is a NEW canonical sync flag. The bridge `sync` subparser accepts and forwards a positive integer. The engine threads `max_changes` through `run_pass` and `reconcile_once` into the existing applier partition seam. `apply_planning._partition_by_mode_cap` uses the explicit cap when present and otherwise uses the retained `MODE_CAPS` value. It never rewrites `Mode` or `MODE_CAPS`. Therefore `bridge sync --max-changes 10` and `100` reproduce bootstrap-strict and bootstrap-throttle mutation caps while legacy `--mode bootstrap-strict` / `bootstrap-throttle` remain byte-for-byte valid for old callers. Invalid, zero, or negative values fail as invocation errors before lock or mutation.\n\n`--only IDS` and `--except IDS` are NEW canonical preview/sync selection flags and are mutually exclusive. They accept non-empty comma-separated local IDs or bound Jira keys. Before lock inspection or acquisition, a read-only selection preflight resolves every supplied local ID or bound Jira key to a stable local-ID set. The preflight reuses `_read_local_tickets(repo_root, no_sync=True)` plus `binding_store.load_binding_store(repo_root)`; both reads avoid ticket-branch sync, snapshot writes, and lock-ref writes. Empty, wholly unresolved, and partially unresolved selections return invocation error 2 at this point. The normalized request then carries only the resolved local-ID set. After the ordinary full snapshots and binding store are loaded under the pass lock but BEFORE any differ runs, the engine uses that already-resolved set to narrow `local_tickets`, `prev_snapshot`, and `curr_snapshot`: `--only` keeps the selected set and `--except` removes it. It does not resolve user input a second time. The existing legacy `--filter-local-ids` path remains separate and unchanged: all differs see full inputs, then only matching mutations reach the applier. Tests must prove the two mechanisms are observably distinct and that neither flag changes unrelated repository/ref state.\n\nSelection helpers live in the existing `reconcile_helpers.py` call-graph seam so `reconcile.py` and `__main__.py` remain below the hard module cap. The explicit cap is passed through `reconcile.py` to `applier.py`, with partition behavior implemented in `apply_planning.py`; no retry, cache, or new lock is introduced.\n\nThe existing external-DC `run_reconcile(mode, only=...)` helper remains a LEGACY compatibility helper and keeps mapping to `--mode` plus post-differ `--filter-local-ids`. Its consumers `_dc_fixtures.py`, `test_dc_mutations.py`, and `test_store_copy_isolation.py` are intentionally unchanged and stay compatibility coverage. A separate additive `run_bridge(...)` helper is used only by explicitly migrated primary-path tests. `tests/interfaces/facades/test_cli.py` remains in the proving set to pin the old reconcile interception/dry-run default and gains canonical dispatch coverage.\n\n## Additional acceptance proof\n\n- [x] `bridge sync --max-changes 10` and `100` apply exactly 10 and 100 deterministic mutations, defer the remainder, and leave legacy bootstrap mode caps unchanged; invalid caps exit 2 before lock or mutation.\n- [x] `bridge preview/sync --only` and `--except` narrow examination before every differ, while legacy `--filter-local-ids` still post-filters writes after full examination; real-entrypoint tests prove distinct plans and unchanged unrelated state.\n- [x] The legacy external-DC helper and all three named consumers retain `--mode` / `--filter-local-ids` behavior; additive primary helpers do not silently migrate compatibility fixtures.\nRepository string census also found `scripts/retarget_jira_project.py` emitting a current operator instruction with `rebar reconcile --mode dry-run`; migrate that PRIMARY guidance to `rebar bridge preview` while leaving compatibility fixtures, historical ADRs, release/migration notes, and explicit old-workflow tests on the retained spelling.\n## Reconcile-check continuity correction\n\nThe workflow mapping above is authoritative: `reconcile-check` is NOT a synonym for preview. it is the retained lock-free diagnostic branch, writes `bridge_state/reconcile-check.json`, and intentionally bypasses the ordinary snapshot/pass path. Until a separately specified primary verb carries that exact diagnostic contract, the production workflow maps only `dry-run` to `rebar bridge preview`; its `reconcile-check` dispatch value continues to invoke `rebar reconcile --mode reconcile-check`. This explicit compatibility use is allowed in the primary workflow because changing it would break the rollback/safe-first-run contract.\n\nThe parity matrix compares every legacy mode with a primary equivalent only where semantics are truly equivalent. It proves reconcile-check unchanged through the retained legacy workflow branch, including no pass-lock acquisition, no snapshot mutation, the same diagnostic artifact/output class, and the same exit behavior. No new primary command claims to replace reconcile-check in this story.\n\n- [x] The migrated production workflow keeps its reconcile-check selection on `rebar reconcile --mode reconcile-check`; dry-run alone maps to `bridge preview`; tests prove the lock-free diagnostic artifact and every old checked-out workflow remain unchanged.\n## Capped-sync deferred-output contract\n\nA capped canonical sync must preserve the rollback-brake audit surface, not merely truncate writes. When `target_mode` is LIVE and explicit `max_changes` is present, `apply_planning._emit_mode_manifest` uses a NEW capped-LIVE branch: it keeps the existing deterministic `_mode_sort_key` ordering, writes and RETAINS the normal manifest, records `max_changes`, lists the full `deferred` remainder (including an empty list when the explicit ceiling is not reached), and returns the manifest path/output class used by capped legacy modes. The presence of an explicit cap, not whether deferral happened, selects this retained audit contract. Uncapped LIVE behavior is unchanged: its temporary manifest is deleted and the applied/failed tally is returned exactly as before. Legacy bootstrap modes and their manifest shapes remain unchanged.\n\nThe cap oracle seeds 102 deterministically ordered candidate mutations. `--max-changes 10` and `100` exercise deferral and assert exact applied identifiers, counts 10/100, deferred counts 92/2, retained manifest path, and unchanged unrelated repository/ref state. `--max-changes 200` exercises an explicit ceiling above the workset and asserts all 102 apply, `deferred == []`, and the audit manifest is still retained. A mutation that removes the explicit-cap branch must make these oracles fail.\n\n- [x] Capped LIVE sync retains a manifest with exact deterministic applied/deferred partitions (10/92 and 100/2 from a 102-mutation fixture) and also retains it with 102/0 when an explicit cap of 200 is not reached; uncapped LIVE still deletes its temporary manifest, and legacy bootstrap output remains unchanged.\n\nAny supplied identifier in `--only` or `--except` that does not resolve to an existing local ID or bound Jira key is an invocation error before lock or mutation, even when other supplied identifiers resolve. The engine never silently substitutes a partially resolved subset. The parity matrix includes mixed known/unknown selections for both flags and asserts rc 2 plus zero repository/ref change.\n\n\n## Subject identity and exact canonical forwarding\n\nThis plan is the plan for story `4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51` itself. It is the downstream\nflag-owning story that prerequisite `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a` names; there is no second story\ncompeting for ownership. The current absence of `--max-changes`, `--only`, and `--except`\nis the forward-looking implementation gap this story closes, not an assertion that those\ncapabilities already exist. The parent epic commits four surviving operational flags across the verb set. This story owns\nthe three preview/sync flags: `--max-changes`, `--only`, and `--except`. The fourth flag,\n`--max-age`, belongs to `bridge status` story `b69c-5787-81b8-4052`; that ticket explicitly\nrequires canonical `bridge status` and compatibility `bridge-status` to share `--max-age`.\nPrerequisite `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a` is corrected to reflect this verb-level ownership.\n\nThe canonical route is built in this change before any workflow uses it:\n`_bridge_commands._parser` adds the preview/sync child options; `bridge_cli` serializes the\nvalidated namespace into engine argv; and `_launch_bridge_command` calls\n`launch_reconciler((command, *child_argv), default_mode=None)`. The launcher is widened so it\ninjects `--mode` only when a legacy adapter supplies a non-null `default_mode`; otherwise it\nforwards canonical `preview` or `sync` plus child options unchanged. New engine\n`request.py` parses either shape into one normalized request before lock acquisition.\n\nConsequently `rebar bridge sync --max-changes 10` reaches normalized\n`request.max_changes == 10` and uses live behavior with an explicit cap, while legacy\n`rebar reconcile --mode bootstrap-strict` still reaches `Mode.BOOTSTRAP_STRICT` and the\nexisting `MODE_CAPS` path. The workflow is migrated in the same Gerrit change only after\nthis parser, forwarding, normalization, partition, and retained-manifest behavior exists, so\nthe workflow never relies on a capability presumed to predate this story.\n\n## Selection preflight ordering correction\n\nThe pre-lock resolver is an explicit new call seam in this story. `request.py` performs\nsyntax and non-empty checks, then `__main__.py` calls a selection resolver in\n`reconcile_helpers.py` before `check_pass_lock`, the phase gate, or\n`acquire_pass_lock`. That resolver reads local tickets with `no_sync=True`, opens the\nbinding store read-only, requires every token to map to an existing local ID, and returns\nonly local IDs. A local ID is valid only when present in the read ticket set; a Jira key is\nvalid only when `BindingStore.get_local_id` maps it to such a ticket. Any missing token,\nincluding one missing member of a mixed list, returns 2 without a lock ref, ticket sync,\nsnapshot update, or mutation.\n\nThe pass receives this resolved set. After its normal locked snapshot/binding load, it\nnarrows all three differ inputs before invoking any differ. This deliberately performs one\nsmall read-only preflight plus the existing pass load: it does not fetch Jira or duplicate\nsnapshot construction before the lock. Tests instrument `check_pass_lock`,\n`acquire_pass_lock`, the fetcher, and repository/ref snapshots to prove unresolved input\nreturns before every one of those effects.\n\n## Durable architecture record\n\nThis story adds `docs/adr/0092-bridge-primary-vocabulary-compatibility-adapters.md` and its\n`docs/adr/.numbers/0092` reservation, then regenerates `docs/adr/README.md`. If 0092 is\nclaimed on the mandatory pre-push refresh, the change takes the next available number and\nregenerates the index rather than colliding.\n\nThe ADR records why the expand-contract migration uses explicit legacy adapters over one\nnormalized request instead of aliases: `reconcile`, direct engine invocation, library\n`reconcile`, and `bridge sync` have different historical defaults; legacy\n`--filter-local-ids` is a post-differ write filter while canonical selection is pre-differ;\nand `reconcile-check` is a lock-free diagnostic rather than preview. It also records the\ncapped-LIVE retained-manifest decision, the separately preserved uncapped/legacy behavior,\nand the later-removal precondition that real consumers first migrate under a separate\ndeprecation decision. Release notes and the architecture documentation link to the ADR.\n\n- [x] The ADR, collision-proof number marker, generated ADR index, and architecture/release\n      links durably capture the distinct-default adapter and capped-manifest rationale; the ADR\n      number checker and generated-index check pass.\n\n## Library and MCP continuity proof\n\nThe affected gate explicitly runs `tests/interfaces/facades/test_library.py` and\n`tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp.py` as compatibility controls. The MCP suite invokes\n`srv.call_tool(\"reconcile\", {\"mode\": \"dry-run\"})` through the real MCP facade and pins\nread-only, mutating-mode refusal, and invalid-mode behavior; the library suite pins the\npublic `rebar.reconcile` surface. These existing tests are not rewritten to canonical\nbridge vocabulary because their purpose in this story is to prove that the old public\nentrypoints remain callable and retain their defaults while the shared engine gains its\nnormalized request path.\n\n- [x] The named library and MCP facade suites pass unchanged and prove legacy\n      `rebar.reconcile` plus MCP `reconcile(mode=...)` remain callable with historical\n      gating/default behavior.\n\n## Max-age verb ownership correction\n\n`--max-age` is not a preview/sync selector and no such existing engine flag is claimed here.\nIt is the stale-liveness assertion on `bridge status`, already specified and tested by\n`b69c-5787-81b8-4052`. Keeping it with status ensures both the canonical and restored legacy\nstatus spellings accept identical options. This story neither invents a duplicate sync\n`--max-age` nor leaves the epic flag unowned.\n\n## Selection staleness under the pass lock\n\nA token that resolved during the read-only preflight can become stale before the pass finishes\nloading its locked inputs because ticket events do not use the reconciler pass lock. After the\nnormal locked load produces `local_tickets`, the engine compares every preflight-resolved local\nID with that loaded local-ID set before invoking any differ. If any target disappeared, both\n`--only` and `--except` fail closed with invocation code 2 and a stale-selection diagnostic\nnaming the missing IDs; the finally path releases the pass lock. No differ, Jira mutation,\nbinding/snapshot persistence, or manifest emission runs. It does not silently intersect to an\nempty selection and does not reinterpret the original token.\n\nThe held-out oracle simulates a target present during preflight and absent from the locked load,\nthen asserts rc 2, the diagnostic, lock release, and zero calls to every differ/applier/persist\nseam.\n\n- [x] A target present during pre-lock resolution but absent from the locked load makes both\n      `--only` and `--except` return 2 with the missing IDs named, releases the lock, and calls no\n      differ, applier, or persistence seam; removing the under-lock recheck fails the held-out\n      oracle.","env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","file_impact":[{"path":".github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml","reason":"migrate the production invocation and rollback input without invalidating old workflow revisions"},{"path":"docs/cli-reference.md","reason":"generate primary bridge documentation while retaining migration guidance"},{"path":"docs/jira-sync-setup.md","reason":"replace primary mode guidance with bridge vocabulary and compatibility mapping"},{"path":"docs/release-notes.md","reason":"record the additive migration, mappings, retained compatibility, and distinct defaults"},{"path":"docs/user-guide.md","reason":"make bridge preview/sync primary and document reconcile compatibility"},{"path":"scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_field_validation_probe.sh","reason":"migrate the in-tree primary invocation while legacy engine flags remain supported"},{"path":"scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_validation_probe.sh","reason":"migrate the in-tree primary invocation while legacy engine flags remain supported"},{"path":"scripts/retarget_jira_project.py","reason":"migrate emitted current operator guidance from reconcile --mode dry-run to canonical bridge preview while compatibility paths remain valid"},{"path":"src/rebar/_cli/__init__.py","reason":"make bridge preview/sync primary while retaining the reconcile adapter and its dry-run default"},{"path":"src/rebar/_cli/_bridge_commands.py","reason":"launch canonical bridge preview/sync through engine subcommands while retaining the legacy launch_reconciler adapter and dry-run default"},{"path":"src/rebar/_cli/help/bridge.txt","reason":"document primary preview/sync options and compatibility mapping without removing old spellings"},{"path":"src/rebar/_cli/help/overview.txt","reason":"make bridge vocabulary primary and document the compatibility mapping"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/__main__.py","reason":"add and normalize primary preview/sync vocabulary while retaining --mode, --filter-local-ids, and the direct-engine live default"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/applier.py","reason":"forward explicit canonical cap to the existing shared partition seam"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_planning.py","reason":"apply explicit max_changes when present and otherwise preserve every MODE_CAPS legacy value"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/mode.py","reason":"compatibility and phase-gate continuity control; MODE_CAPS and Mode must remain"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/reconcile.py","reason":"thread explicit max_changes and canonical selection scope through the existing pass seam without changing legacy filter_local_ids"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/reconcile_helpers.py","reason":"host pre-differ only/except scope resolution along the existing helper call-graph seam"},{"path":"src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/request.py","reason":"new extracted normalized-request parser for primary subcommands and max-changes/only/except while preserving legacy --mode defaults below the module cap"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_fixtures.py","reason":"continuity census: unchanged consumer of legacy run_reconcile post-filter semantics"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_support.py","reason":"migrate the primary caller while retaining compatibility coverage"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/conftest.py","reason":"migrate documentation and fixtures to primary vocabulary"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_dc_mutations.py","reason":"continuity census: unchanged consumer of legacy run_reconcile post-filter semantics"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_reconcile_pass.py","reason":"exercise reconciler behavior through the primary vocabulary"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_store_copy_isolation.py","reason":"continuity census: preserve documented post-filter semantics while primary helper is additive"},{"path":"tests/external/live_jira_dc/test_user_guide_commands.py","reason":"validate current documented commands and legacy continuity"},{"path":"tests/interfaces/facades/test_bridge_cli_heldout.py","reason":"real subprocess parity for canonical engine subcommands and legacy CLI/default continuity"},{"path":"tests/interfaces/facades/test_cli.py","reason":"retain reconcile interception and dry-run default coverage plus canonical bridge dispatch proof"},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_mode_cap_enforcement.py","reason":"prove explicit 10/100 caps and unchanged legacy MODE_CAPS behavior"},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_filter.py","reason":"prove distinct legacy --filter-local-ids and primary --only semantics"},{"path":"tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_main.py","reason":"prove primary and compatibility entrypoint parity including distinct defaults"},{"path":"tests/unit/test_gen_cli_reference.py","reason":"pin generated primary bridge reference plus retained compatibility documentation"},{"path":"tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py","reason":"migrate production invocation assertions while preserving paused commit-skip and legacy numeric compatibility mapping until 9895"}],"file_impact_scope":"paths","keyring":[],"last_status_env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","managed_refs":[["depends_on","4752-3afb-e8bd-448a"],["parent","2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3"],["relates_to","2fb7-c90e-6a1d-4b82"],["relates_to","659a-14eb-5692-4152"],["relates_to","9062-b26e-c96f-4622"]],"no_file_impact_reason":"","parent_id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","parent_status_uuid":"d5d415a5-9cfc-4889-95e6-4946296213ab","plan_review_phase":"execution","preconditions_summary":{"status":"pre-manifest"},"priority":1,"reverts":[],"status":"in_progress","tags":["bridge","ux"],"ticket_id":"4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51","ticket_type":"story","title":"Make bridge preview/sync primary while preserving reconcile compatibility","verify_commands":[]},"source_event_uuids":["a24fc239-d2bd-42b3-b6f7-e813b7c23fe3","c0c1fa48-73e7-4c98-8001-ba297acb4b26","21ef27a9-5062-46dd-95fd-b1969c80226f","59b0d774-2bc6-4b3f-94f4-06466350a4f4","10b3c471-d6da-4d6a-bfb6-778e50b968bc","e82bc0b6-2129-45cb-aa7d-89e081d3ceb6","caa3ede4-1caf-4503-ab5d-0e4f1e391d94","597cb864-34f3-4061-9cfd-ed79093afce4","a0dac516-2bbf-4b13-8728-5d45f4c64103","c3584fb2-bb5f-4baa-9b9d-beebd8e1deb1","b3c5fb8f-a257-4bcc-8ab7-6fd4deb3dfca","d9cea96b-8754-4386-9a65-2608456437d0","202bbd55-9757-4680-92d2-cd944edc7a88","dec24b2a-022c-4e9c-b4a0-538cc4af3b91","43f31771-43d8-4bf4-8c79-4f7b157866bc","9da07b32-bdb8-48bc-9232-e08a31f5d52b","2f4de8bc-aa81-4902-9227-3c3401e117c9","f9fc3c2c-cdcf-4c35-bbd1-0b340d8aeced","0cc0b1b4-6501-450c-97a5-1ceebadf14e7","6f417a5f-0073-4a5b-8172-d58540a78efd","8192c5de-7a19-460c-a164-50656cc17d50","10eaff4f-a1ac-4133-8f3e-a87e4fe04bf2","6e1a269a-06d2-4b0d-aff3-0df0418d1cb2","3d33473a-3c5d-4935-97aa-19b8341bc51d","f88e9e1b-f3e5-496e-9b81-3cbcccfced57","c2ad03de-0485-4f27-9167-0cd3d6ba7d43","28cb0589-b781-4633-9871-b57f46f850be","a8794bb5-866d-49e4-92f7-cb95f4c223de","d5d415a5-9cfc-4889-95e6-4946296213ab","1702608f-34d2-40de-96d8-f69bff3252c4","d43f186a-341e-49cb-b15f-302d26c1ef4a","1643f385-9e33-4df8-a450-cd10ea71c9f9","1f264d97-a9cd-49bb-a4f8-ecf1b34e29b2","b4502158-0eff-4518-b1da-54ae39fe6e33","a9049f6c-c515-4e2c-b10a-490c507253c0","af842a90-89d2-4d6f-ade4-3bff1c3a205a","930b55c3-490f-42b7-8a4b-f9d44df39695","5ff154ef-8e65-445f-b929-4866666a36ab","dd420a5c-838e-4987-bfc8-454a0d7ed9e9"]},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"SNAPSHOT","timestamp":1786233524325866002,"uuid":"7accfb89-8e4d-4dbc-92ba-aacf2e227d43"}